WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.190
- This video was made
possible by Tab for a Cause.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:05.430
Raise money for charity
just by opening new tabs

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.090
which you were going to do anyway.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.000
The coronavirus is wrecking
havoc on the world.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.760
Gone is the basketball
season, the summer Olympics,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.540
and, unfortunately, baseball.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.710
But at least the Nationals

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.460
will remain World Series
champions for another year.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:20.700
So many things are getting canceled

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.160
that people are wondering

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.110
what else could possibly be canceled

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.260
before this thing is over.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.880
Particularly, people are worried

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.170
about the upcoming Presidential election.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.230
Once considered completely unthinkable,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.590
people are now legitimately
asking this question,

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:35.470
especially since this has already impacted

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.550
the political process.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.791
Already several democratic
primaries have been postponed,

00:00:38.791 --> 00:00:42.510
even within hours of their
scheduled primary date.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.790
For example, Ohio Governor Mike DeWine

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.600
actually asked the State Supreme Court

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.280
to postpone their primary election.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.460
When the Ohio Supreme
Court denied his request

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.300
he simply ordered the polls closed,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.620
effectively circumventing
the court's decision.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.980
- This is not about politics.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.120
This is about protecting
Ohio's citizen's lives.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.200
- So if it's possible to
postpone a primary election,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.530
can the general election
be postponed as well?

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.400
It's natural to wonder if President Trump

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.600
already has a legally
plausible plan up his sleeve

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.600
to just cancel the election entirely

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.400
and start a 2nd term in office unelected.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.950
So can the president

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.180
actually cancel the general
election this November?

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.013
The answer will surprise you.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.930
(orchestral music)

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.490
Hey Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:31.490
because our democracy is at
stake, or is it? (eagle caws)

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.950
There's certainly a lot of uncertainty

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.060
about the November election

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.226
and that was even before
the current pandemic.

00:01:36.226 --> 00:01:39.760
Will we be standing in line
to vote with masks and gloves

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.710
or will we be able to mail in our vote

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.640
or will President Trump just take over

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.520
and become the dear leader for life

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.710
as all true Americans want him to?

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.050
Well, today we're going
to discuss whether or not

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.560
the president can actually
cancel the general election

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.330
and all of the wacky legal
implications that this has.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.450
Full disclosure, some of
the issues in the analysis

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.240
in this episode come from the
podcast Opening Arguments.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.050
Opening Arguments is a great podcast

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.710
run by Attorney Andrew
Torrez and Thomas Smith.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.410
Andrew took a deep dive into this subject

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.010
in response to a thought experiment

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.870
by Vox journalist Ian Millhiser.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.260
I'll drop a link to the
Opening Arguments podcast

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:13.180
in the doobly-doo.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.270
Definitely check it
out, it's really great.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.720
So the question is can Trump
cancel the general election?

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.650
Now look, I'm not going to bury the lead

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.340
and make you sweat
through this entire video

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:23.340
to get to the answer.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.173
The bottom line is

00:02:24.173 --> 00:02:27.150
the president cannot
legally cancel the election.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.010
It probably won't happen.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.192
Now, could he try?

00:02:30.192 --> 00:02:33.140
Well sure, anyone can try anything

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.978
just like anyone can sue
another person over anything

00:02:35.978 --> 00:02:38.530
but it doesn't mean that
they're going to be successful.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.460
But no matter what way you slice it,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.020
even with President
Trump's ever expansive view

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.930
of his Article II presidential powers.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.930
- Then I have an Article II

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.760
where I have the right to do
whatever I want as president

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.620
but I don't even talk about that.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.280
- Even he can't take steps

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.150
that would effectively
morph his presidency

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.360
into a dictatorship.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.700
That's because the Constitution itself

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.935
places specific parameters
on presidential terms,

00:03:00.935 --> 00:03:03.330
including the length of a president's term

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.340
as well as the specific date and time

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.560
as to when that term ends.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.010
But that's just the beginning

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.980
of the insanity that would happen

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.490
if the election were in fact canceled,

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.910
and in this context I'm
using the word canceled

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.270
to mean the president actually puts troops

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.680
and prevents people from
actually going to the polls

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.470
because he can't legally
cancel the election,

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.380
so we're talking about some
really drastic moves here.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.357
Now, that being said
the Constitution states,

00:03:28.357 --> 00:03:30.347
"The President shall hold his office

00:03:30.347 --> 00:03:32.200
"during the term of four years."

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.240
And even more specifically it
states in the 20th Amendment

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:36.073
that his term,

00:03:36.073 --> 00:03:39.510
"shall end at noon on
the 20th day of January."

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.510
Very specific, not much wiggle room.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.180
Furthermore, the President is elected

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.590
by each of the state's
electors under Article II

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.150
of the Constitution
and Congress determines

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.600
the time of choosing of the electors

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.080
and the day on which they
shall give their votes.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.230
Remember, the President it
elected not by popular vote

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:57.980
but by the electoral college

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.370
and you'll see later in this video

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.860
even if President Trump opts out

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.810
to become a squatter in the White House,

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.710
legally the presidency
would pass to somebody else,

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:10.730
effective on the 29th of January, 2021.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.960
Now, I could already hear
you saying in the comments,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.420
great, thanks for the quick
constitutional law lesson

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.340
but regardless of what
the Constitution says,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.580
could the President
still find some shady way

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.710
to use the coronavirus to his advantage

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:24.900
to postpone or possibly
cancel the November election.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.741
For instance, can the
president declare martial law

00:04:27.741 --> 00:04:30.740
due to the pandemic and legally
circumvent the Constitution?

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.920
Well, the answer to this and
any other similar question

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.230
is mostly no.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.322
More specifically, when it
comes to the Constitution

00:04:37.322 --> 00:04:39.070
there's no known circumstance

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.700
that can suspend the
enforcement of the Constitution,

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.830
not a pandemic, not a
natural disaster, not a war,

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.710
and not even a war fought between citizens

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.500
of the same country,
specifically the Civil War,

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.240
and believe me, it was tried.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.190
And that was the case in the
famous constitutional law case

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.900
of Ex parte Milligan
which is the seminal case

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.620
for simply saying that the
Constitution is the Constitution

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.870
and no matter the circumstances,
the Constitution applies.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.940
Ex parte Milligan was
born out of the events

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.250
surrounding the Civil War.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.995
Like a pandemic, a civil war
is an unprecedented event

00:05:08.995 --> 00:05:13.030
and President Lincoln suspended
the writ of habeas corpus,

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.520
which was an unconstitutional event.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.300
So the first question is in this context,

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.240
what is a writ of habeas corpus?

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.820
A writ of habeas corpus, as
you've probably seen in movies,

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.780
is a federal tool uses to bring
a prisoner before the court

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.440
to determine if their imprisonment
or detention is lawful.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:30.660
The drafters of the Constitution

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.560
actually considered a
writ of habeas corpus

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:33.970
to be so important

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.830
that they included the concept
of petitioning the court

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.530
for a release of a prisoner
who's unlawfully detained

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.610
in the first Article of
the actual US Constitution,

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.810
not in the Bill of Rights,
not in the Amendments,

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.364
but in the actual Constitution itself.

00:05:48.364 --> 00:05:50.187
Now, Article I states that,

00:05:50.187 --> 00:05:52.097
"The privileges of the
writ of habeas corpus

00:05:52.097 --> 00:05:53.560
"shall not be suspended unless

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:56.351
"when in cases of rebellion or invasion

00:05:56.351 --> 00:05:58.554
"the public safety may require it."

00:05:58.554 --> 00:06:01.160
President Lincoln suspended
the writ of habeas corpus

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.400
at the same time that
he declared martial law.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.181
Martial law is basically the law

00:06:05.181 --> 00:06:07.240
administered by military forces

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.810
that is invoked by a
government in an emergency

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.550
when the civilian law enforcement agencies

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.140
are unable to maintain
public order and safety.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:19.140
And by the way, it's martial
law spelled M-A-R-T-I-A-L

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.037
not Marshall spelled M-A-R-S-H-A-L-L.

00:06:23.037 --> 00:06:28.037
This is not some guy named
Marshall, it's martial as in war,

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.310
war-like law, it's just
a pet peeve of mine

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:34.770
that I see people misspelling
all the time on social media.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.970
Anyway, notwithstanding, the
facts of ex parte Milligan

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:40.630
involved a racist lawyer by
the name of Lambdin P. Milligan

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:43.600
who was charged with conspiracy
against the United States.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:44.793
aiding and abetting the confederacy,

00:06:44.793 --> 00:06:46.690
and inciting insurrections.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.628
Now, when Lincoln suspended
the writ of habeas corpus

00:06:49.628 --> 00:06:51.580
and declared martial law,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.980
the government tried to
circumvent the courts

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.930
and said that Milligan
could be legally tried

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:57.200
in a military tribunal

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.371
instead of through the
civilian court system

00:06:59.371 --> 00:07:04.130
where he was quickly convicted
and sentenced to hang.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:06.020
But, before Milligan could be hanged

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.540
he sued to stay the execution

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.240
and the Supreme Court agreed with him

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.606
saying that you can't
suspend habeas corpus.

00:07:11.606 --> 00:07:13.910
The Supreme Court held that
it was unconstitutional

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:15.822
to try civilians by military tribunal

00:07:15.822 --> 00:07:18.668
unless there were no
civilian courts available,

00:07:18.668 --> 00:07:20.450
which wasn't the case.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.170
In support of the Supreme Court's holding,

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.290
the court noted, and
here's the important part,

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.350
that citizens have certain
rights by the Constitution

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:27.890
such as the trial by jury

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:30.267
and that this constitutional
guaranty was quote,

00:07:30.267 --> 00:07:33.467
"intended for a state of war
as well as a state of peace

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:36.107
"and is equally binding
upon rulers and people

00:07:36.107 --> 00:07:38.389
"at all times and under
all circumstances."

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:40.910
Bottom line, if Lincoln couldn't
get around the Constitution

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.550
while Americans were literally
at war with each other,

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.820
then President Trump
doesn't stand a chance

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.510
of being able to step outside the confines

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:50.450
of the Constitution due to
the coronavirus pandemic.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.290
But, could President
Trump try to reschedule

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:55.730
or postpone indefinitely the election?

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:57.920
Well, as President Trump
is finding out the hard way

00:07:57.920 --> 00:07:59.380
regarding emergency powers,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:02.370
a lot of election power
resides with the states.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.747
Article I states that, "The
times, places, and manner

00:08:04.747 --> 00:08:07.127
"of holding elections for
senators and representatives

00:08:07.127 --> 00:08:08.757
"shall be prescribed in each state

00:08:08.757 --> 00:08:11.887
"by the legislature thereof,
but the Congress may

00:08:11.887 --> 00:08:14.637
"at any time by law make
or alter such regulations

00:08:14.637 --> 00:08:18.010
"except as to the places
of choosing of senators."

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.240
So election law is really up to the states

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.470
with a little bit of oversight

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.040
from Congress and the Constitution.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.870
Federal laws have long been on the books

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.080
that dictate when the
President, Vice President,

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.430
and members of Congress are elected.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:31.247
You can look to three US C
one, which states that quote,

00:08:31.247 --> 00:08:33.207
"The electors of the
president and vice president

00:08:33.207 --> 00:08:34.817
"shall be appointed in each state,

00:08:34.817 --> 00:08:38.007
"on the Tuesday after the
first Monday in November,

00:08:38.007 --> 00:08:40.477
"in every fourth year
succeeding every election

00:08:40.477 --> 00:08:42.460
"of a President and Vice President."

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.270
Similar laws set the date
senators and congresspeople

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.600
start their term, the
third day of January.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.540
These laws are incredibly specific

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:51.900
and nothing in them provides any exception

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.930
for the President to
move these dates around.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.880
And since we have federal
statutes in place,

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:57.300
people should have solace

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.540
that the President cannot
simply reschedule the election.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.047
As democratic presidential
candidate Joe Biden put,

00:09:02.047 --> 00:09:03.537
"We voted in the middle of the Civil War,

00:09:03.537 --> 00:09:06.257
"we voted in the middle of
World War I and II," and so

00:09:06.257 --> 00:09:08.672
"The idea of postponing the
electoral process," it just,

00:09:08.672 --> 00:09:10.390
"seems to me out of the question."

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:12.180
- This is about making sure

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.059
that we're able to conduct our democracy

00:09:14.059 --> 00:09:16.320
while we're dealing with a pandemic.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.217
- So, now we've established
the Constitution

00:09:18.217 --> 00:09:19.840
and federal statutes are specific

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.380
about when the elections take place

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:22.950
and when the president's term ends.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.840
Both the Constitution and federal statutes

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.590
can only be changed by a
subsequent act of Congress

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:29.020
to amend them, and the president

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.090
can't take any unilateral action

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:33.330
to supersede them independently.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.670
But President Trump has
repeatedly stated his belief

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.081
that Article II gives him absolute power.

00:09:38.081 --> 00:09:40.720
- It's a thing called Article II.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:43.050
Nobody ever mentions Article II.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.780
It gives me all of these rights

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.110
at a level that nobody
has ever seen before.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:49.814
We don't even talk about Article II.

00:09:49.814 --> 00:09:52.620
- So what if in the coming
months President Trump thinks

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.370
that he might lose the general election?

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:56.570
What could President Trump do?

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.650
Well, there are basically two
things that President Trump

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.468
could attempt to do to remain in office.

00:10:00.468 --> 00:10:02.530
He could try to convince the states

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:05.270
to take away their citizen's
right to vote for president

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.560
or he could try to use the military

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:10.080
to physically prevent people from voting.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.250
The first scenario is much more plausible;

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.090
though, still very, very unlikely.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:16.570
Because the 50 states
hold considerable power

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:18.380
in determining the rules for elections,

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.850
including federal elections,

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.610
then that happens to take
place within their borders.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:24.510
The states can control
the method of voting

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.343
that will be used,

00:10:25.343 --> 00:10:27.740
whether or not a felon can
vote for example, and what,

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.880
if any, identification voters
much show at the polls.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.100
Though, of course there are
some constitutional limitations

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:34.860
to those kind of restrictions,

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.920
namely from the 15th Amendment
and the Voting Rights Act

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.440
which deal with ensuring that
citizens aren't prevented

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.789
from voting due to discriminatory
or arbitrary means.

00:10:43.789 --> 00:10:44.880
But other than that

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.390
the elections are mostly under
the control of the states

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:49.380
and, if forced,

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:51.550
potentially states could
find alternative ways

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.560
to hold congressional or
presidential elections

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.220
with each state
secretively scheduling them

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.430
outside of the designated
federal election day.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.930
Or conceivably if people
are physically prevented

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:02.753
from going to the polls no matter what,

00:11:02.753 --> 00:11:04.963
then conceivably the state legislatures

00:11:04.963 --> 00:11:08.490
could take back the
power of their electors

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:13.190
in the electoral college and
vote in the electoral college

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.570
the way that they think
that their state population

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:18.080
would vote in the general election.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.430
Which, of course, would
be incredibly ironic

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.720
because it would actually be a good use

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.554
for the electoral college,
which under normal circumstances

00:11:25.554 --> 00:11:27.990
there are simply no good defenses

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.010
for the electoral college
in this day and age

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.410
to thwart the popular vote.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:34.247
- Two thumbs down.

00:11:34.247 --> 00:11:37.052
- And anyone who tries to
defend the electoral college

00:11:37.052 --> 00:11:41.460
is simply wrong, and/or
dumb, but I digress.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.590
So, even if the president
successfully, albeit illegally,

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:47.880
thwarts the election and
electors never cast their vote

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:49.720
or their votes are never counted,

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.970
there are constitutional
provisions already in place

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.000
that we discussed that
would prevent his term

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.480
from naturally extending.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.900
So, if the election doesn't go forward

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.580
then President Trump doesn't
just get to remain in office.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:02.930
To find out what would happen

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.610
we'd just have to go
back to our conversation

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:07.190
at the beginning of this
video, which if you'll recall

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.820
is the fact that the president
shall hold his office

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:11.960
during the term of four years

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.930
and specifically the 20th Amendment,

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.950
which provides for an
end date of that term

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.580
which provides that the
president's term shall expire

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.750
on noon on the 20th day of January.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:24.760
So, if there is no election,

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:28.950
then by default the president's
term ends on January 20th.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.770
One way or another he's
out of the White House.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.010
This doesn't depend on
President Trump's willingness

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:35.130
to vacate the White House.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:37.240
Legally, he is no longer the president

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.054
and I suspect if he stayed there,

00:12:39.054 --> 00:12:42.487
there would be some big
men with green uniforms

00:12:42.487 --> 00:12:44.330
and big pointy guns

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:46.640
that would remove him
from the White House.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.950
- Unless they give me an
extension for the presidency.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:54.120
- But if President Trump isn't
the president then who is?

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.530
Let's play this scenario out
just a little bit further

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:58.820
because we've established
that President Trump

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:02.590
is no longer the president
come January 20, 2021.

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:05.080
But that still begs the question, who is?

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:06.850
Now, I'll tell you who
would become president

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:08.960
but first I wanna tell you
who should become president,

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:10.180
that's Jose Andres.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:13.001
Jose Andres is basically the
patron saint of DC these days.

00:13:13.001 --> 00:13:15.060
The world-famous chef created something

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.470
called the World Central Kitchen

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:19.450
to feed those in need during
natural and manmade disasters.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:22.190
They were on the ground in
Puerto Rico for Hurricane Maria

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:23.910
and they're out in the
streets helping people

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:25.300
during this pandemic.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.040
They've already served
over 15 million meals.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.530
I just gave $500 and it
would mean the world to me

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:31.960
that if you're in a position to give,

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:33.970
if you could give a little bit as well.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:35.800
The link to the website
is in the description,

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.230
every little bit helps,
and it would be great

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:39.120
if we could come together

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.840
and help people during
this coronavirus pandemic.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.510
But, the question is who
would become president

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:47.840
in this crazy situation
where there was no election?

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.810
Under normal circumstances,
the constitution

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:52.440
and the federal statutes
provide an order of succession

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.370
for who would take over
the powers and duties

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.980
of the office of president in the event

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.080
the current president is
incapacitated, dies, resigns,

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:01.450
or is removed from office

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:03.650
or in this case his term
has effectively ended,

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:05.990
and you may recall under
this line of succession,

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.230
which we have covered a
couple times on this channel,

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:09.941
under Article II of the Constitution

00:14:09.941 --> 00:14:13.110
after the president it
goes to the vice president

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:15.550
and then precedes next to
the speaker of the house,

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.820
the president pro tempore
of the Senate and so forth

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:20.490
under the Presidential Succession Act.

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:21.600
Now first things first,

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:23.940
we can eliminate some of these options

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:25.434
because under this hypothetical

00:14:25.434 --> 00:14:27.980
there hasn't been an election.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:32.360
And so just as the president's
term expires on January 20th

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:35.320
so too does the vice
president's term expire.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.460
So the vice president
can't pick up the mantle

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:41.400
for the president whose term
has expired on the same day.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:44.890
And then next, we can't
necessarily give the presidency

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:47.880
to the speaker of the
house because remember,

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:51.850
members of Congress are
reelected every two years.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:53.816
So every time there's
a presidential election

00:14:53.816 --> 00:14:57.200
all of the congresspeople
are also reelected.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.210
So if there is no general election

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.899
then there aren't going to
be any members of Congress

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:04.170
in the House of Representatives.

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:05.760
There might be some people in the Senate

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.170
because they're elected on
a six-year staggered basis

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:11.180
but there would be no members of Congress,

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:12.080
for the most part.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.990
So this creates a bunch
of really, really weird

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:16.810
Constitutional scenarios,

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:17.990
but we know a couple things for certain.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.440
Number one, we know that the
president's term expires,

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.800
so that president can't remain president.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.820
Number two, vice president's
term has also expired.

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:28.700
We know that there aren't
any members of Congress,

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:30.310
presumably, at least in the first instance

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:31.690
so there's no speaker of the house.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:34.560
So that leaves a couple
of really weird scenarios.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.053
Number one is that if
these state legislatures

00:15:37.053 --> 00:15:39.080
or the state governors got together

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.400
and just simply agreed to reappoint

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:44.780
all of the members of Congress,
then you have a sitting body

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:46.100
in the House of Representatives.

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:48.310
If that's the case, then President Trump

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:50.480
would have to swallow hard
when he finds out that,

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:54.030
that means his archenemy Speaker
of the House Nancy Pelosi

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.810
would now be filling his shoes

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:58.030
as the third in line of succession.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.790
Now, the other option would
be that those in Congress

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:04.120
whose term was set to expire
would not be reappointed,

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:07.110
which would completely abolish
the House of Representatives,

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:08.080
at least in the short term,

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:09.993
since they're all voted
on every two years,

00:16:09.993 --> 00:16:14.100
and at that point what we have
is 2/3 of the senators left

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.160
since 2/3 of the senators
weren't up for reelection

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.320
but 1/3 were up for reelection.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.330
So in this scenario, we have no president,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:22.940
no vice president, no
speaker of the house,

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.430
and no members of the
House of Representatives.

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.660
What that means then is we
would go to the fourth person

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:30.300
in the line of succession

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:32.260
which would be the president pro tempore

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.460
of the Senate as the acting president

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:36.580
because they're next
in line for succession.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:37.930
Now, you can be forgiven

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:40.310
if you have forgotten
your high school civics.

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:42.300
The president pro tempore of the senate

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:45.840
is generally the oldest serving member

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:48.320
of the majority party in the Senate

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:52.430
and in this case that would
be Senator Chuck Grassly,

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:55.360
the republican senator
who is 85 years young

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:57.280
from the great state of Iowa

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:01.370
who is currently the president
pro tempore of the Senate.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:04.540
So, does that answer the
question, President Chuck Grassly?

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:07.250
Well, probably not because remember I said

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:10.960
that 1/3 of the senators are
actually up for reelection

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:12.630
in this election cycle.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.420
So I don't think that the state governors

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.500
are just going to sit idly by.

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:20.010
They would probably
appoint interim senators

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.957
to fill the gaps left by the senators

00:17:23.957 --> 00:17:26.200
who were up for reelection.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:30.270
And so then you have to look
at how many of the vacancies

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.070
would be filled by republican governors

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.680
and how many vacancies would be filled

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:35.100
by democratic senators?

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.130
And thankfully the math
has already been done

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:39.470
by Andrew at Opening Arguments

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:43.500
who figured out that there
are more democratic governors

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.610
who would fill vacancies
than republican governors

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:47.670
who would fill vacancies.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:49.450
So, at the end of the day,

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:51.817
of the senators who are up for reelection

00:17:51.817 --> 00:17:55.140
and are being reappointed,
that probably means

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:58.950
that there would be a democratic
majority in the senate,

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:01.040
which means that the president pro tempore

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.259
would be nominated by the democrats

00:18:04.259 --> 00:18:08.500
and the senior most democratic
senator in the Senate

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:13.180
is none other than
Vermont Senator Pat Leahy.

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:18.180
So, we can all welcome President
Pat Leahy to the office.

00:18:18.595 --> 00:18:19.610
So there ya go.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.650
In the wildest of twists,

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:24.080
the most likely outcomes
if President Trump

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:25.680
were to somehow cancel the election

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:30.680
is either President Nancy
Pelosi or President Pat Leahy.

00:18:30.745 --> 00:18:32.630
I don't really know how to feel

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:34.649
about either of those two scenarios.

00:18:34.649 --> 00:18:36.900
But after all of these crazy hypotheticals

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.570
and rabbit holes that we've gone through,

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:40.410
I'll say one more thing
to ease everyone's mind.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.120
There is no plausible way

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:43.680
in which the president could cancel

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.950
or even postpone the election
and remain president.

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:49.890
We have held presidential
elections in multiple world wars,

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:52.520
in civil wars, in past pandemics,

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.590
and even when the White House
was burned to the ground

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:56.430
by the British.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:57.730
As we've done in the past,

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:01.350
we will elect our next president
the good old fashioned way

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.810
and we will not have to
deal with a President Pelosi

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:05.390
or President Leahy.

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:07.920
Now, if you're waiting to see
what happens to our democracy

